<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-12" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Energy Diagrams</title>


  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
        The Morse Potential,
        <m>
            ~{U(x)= D(e^{-2a(x-x_0)}-2e^{-a(x-x_0)})}
        </m>, is a useful model to describe the interaction between two atoms. 
        Here, <m>x</m> represents the separation between the two atoms, which have a reduced mass
        <m>\mu</m>. A graph of <m>U(x)</m> is shown below.
        </p>
        <image>
            <shortdescription>descr</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \begin{axis}[
                    axis y line = left,
                    axis x line = middle,
                    xtick = {0},
                    xmin=0,xmax=6.2,
                    ymin=-1.5,ymax=2.5,
                    xlabel = \(x\),
                    ylabel = {\(U/D\)},
                    xlabel style={anchor=west},
                    every axis plot/.append style={ultra thick}
                ]

                \addplot [
                    domain=0:6, 
                    samples=100, 
                    color=red,
                ]
                {exp(-2*(x-1))-2*exp(-(x-1))};

                \end{axis}
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find the location of the stable equilibrium point.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    For small values of <m>x</m>,
                    <m>e^{x}\simeq 1 + x + x^{2}/2 + (...)</m>.
                    Use this approximation to find the <q>effective spring 
                    constant</q> for this interaction.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What is the vibration frequency for small oscillations around the
                    equilibrium point?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Using the graph, what is the <alert>dissociation energy</alert> of
                    the molecule?
                    This is the amount of energy you have to add to the 
                    two-atom system, when it is at the stable equilibrium, 
                    for the molecule to break apart. 
                    <q>Break apart</q> here means that the atoms can be at 
                    infinite separation from one another.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Note a limitation of this model, at least thinking of atoms as
                    little hard spheres: if you give enough kinetic energy to the
                    molecule, the molecular separation can go to 0.
                    How much kinetic energy is necessary for that to happen, assuming
                    the atoms start off very far away from one another?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
